<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tekle Haymanot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1215–<a href="/years/1313">1313</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Takla Haymanot</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tekle Haymanot was an Ethiopian monk and saint, one of the most revered figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox Church</a>. Saints' lives describe him preaching in the pagan kingdom of Damot and founding the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-libanos">Debre Libanos</a> in Shewa, which became the leading monastic house of the Ethiopian church. He was later credited with helping to restore the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a> in 1270.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tekle-haymanot-saint.jpg" alt="Tekle Haymanot" width="320" height="453" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abune_Tekele_Haymanot.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tekle Haymanot was born in Zorare, a district of Selale on the eastern edge of Shewa, into an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> family of the long-established Christian population of northern Shewa. His hagiographical tradition traces his ancestors' migration south from the old Aksumite lands through Bete Amhara, a movement the historian Taddesse Tamrat places within the wider southward spread of Christian settlers after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a>'s decline. He was the son of the priest Tsega Zeab and his wife Egzi'e Haraya, also called Sarah, who according to tradition had pledged their firstborn to God.</p><p>In his youth Shewa suffered raids by Motolomi Sato, the pagan king of Damot beyond the Jamma River, and several traditions tell of his dealings with that king. His father gave him his first religious teaching; he was made a deacon at fifteen and was later ordained a priest by Pope Cyril III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At about thirty he went north to study, spending nine years under Iyasus Mo'a at the island monastery of Lake Hayq and then staying at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-damo">Debre Damo</a> under Abbot Yohannes after visiting Axum. Tradition holds that he received the full monastic habit on his return journey at Lake Hayq; Taddesse Tamrat reads this account as a later attempt to assert that monastery's seniority over his followers.</p><p>Back in Shewa he settled between Selale and Grarya, where in 1284 he founded the monastery of Debre Atsbo, renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-libanos">Debre Libanos</a> in the 15th century. Its abbot, the Ichege, came to rank second only to the Abuna. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> promoted the saint's veneration and the monastery's standing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He lived 29 years after the foundation and was first buried in the cave where he had lived as a hermit; almost 60 years later he was reburied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-libanos">Debre Libanos</a>, where Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> built a new church over the tomb in the 1950s. He is often shown as an old man with wings and one visible leg, after stories of his standing in prayer until a leg broke. Historians such as Taddesse Tamrat and G.W.B. Huntingford regard his role in the Solomonic restoration as a later invention, since older traditions credit Iyasus Mo'a. His feast falls on 30 August, and hagiographies of him were written from the 15th century onward.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tekle Haymanot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its people followed traditional religions, and saints' lives describe the missionary <b>Tekle Haymanot</b>, founder of Debre Libanos, preaching there.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-damot">Kingdom of Damot</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1881 Emperor Yohannes IV crowned Ras Adal Tessema, the ruler of Gojjam, as Negus <b>Tekle Haymanot</b> of Gojjam and Kaffa, partly to balance the power of Menelik of the Kingdom of Shewa.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gojjam">Kingdom of Gojjam</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shewa, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-libanos">Debre Libanos</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=9,39&amp;year=1278" aria-label="Shewa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tekle Haymanot"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5757 4233h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5757 4233h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=9,39&amp;year=1278">What was here in 1278? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
